  "worked_solution": "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- Function $f(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is not continuous at $t=0.$ Indeed, the left and right limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to +0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=+\\infty  \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to -0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ doesn’t exist.\n\n- Function $g(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\frac{2}{t}$ is not continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- Function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components $2t$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and ${t}^{4}$ are continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $h(t)$ only.",
